; The device --simulate starts, and the description the run binds for it.
;
; One file drives both sides on purpose: the simulated device is built FROM this description, and
; the same file is bound as the peer description, so the device and the file agree by construction.
; A --simulate run therefore exercises the eds group and everything the peer-SDO gate would
; otherwise block, which is the whole point of having a dry run — a smoke test that skipped half
; the checks would test half the runner.
;
; It is deliberately a separate file from tests/CanKit.Pro.Tests/.../Fixtures/device.eds, which it
; started as. That one is evidence for the device-description tests and changing it changes what
; they measure; this one exists to be exercised, and carries 1010h/1011h and a synchronous TPDO2
; that those tests have no reason to want.
;
; Covers: the three mandatory objects, SYNC producer and cycle, heartbeat producer and consumer,
; EMCY, node guarding, store/restore, one RPDO, an event-driven TPDO1 and a synchronous TPDO2,
; and three application objects.

